  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gradually warming temperatures through Monday with increasing\n precipitation chances each day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMultiple shortwave-troughs will traverse the region during the\nfirst half of next week bringing increasing chances of precip.\n\nAir mass remains pretty stable this afternoon per NUCAPS data\nfrom 1744Z. Really don't see much developing today, except for a\nspotty shower here and there west of I-81.\n\nAtmosphere becomes a bit more unstable Sunday and Monday aided\nby mid-level cooling and increasing warmth and instability and\nforcing from an approaching shortwave-trough. Should see a \ngradual uptick in convection each afternoon through Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Returning to a summer-like pattern next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nGlobal ensemble models agree on a highly amplified pattern across \nthe continent. In particular, a sprawling upper ridge is likely to \ndominate the Desert Southwest into the Great Plains. At the same \ntime, below average heights will persist along eastern sections of \nNorth America. While the core of lower heights stays in close \nproximity to Hudson Bay, an elongated longwave trough maintains its \npresence along the Eastern Seaboard. This configuration would \nsupport a mainly northwesterly flow regime aloft with embedded \ndisturbances sweeping up and over the prominent central U.S. ridge.\n\nTuesday still presents itself as the most active day of the period \nas the calendar closes on the month of July. Although ensembles \nsomewhat wash out the signal of this shortwave, deterministic \nsolutions depict this sharpening system encroaching on the area by \nTuesday into Tuesday night. Aided by modest height/pressure falls, a \ndeep wave of low pressure is expected to track from the Ohio Valley \ntoward the Delmarva region. Ample instability and vertical shear are \nin play for an active day of severe weather, but at the same time is \naccompanied by many of the usual question marks. Many global \nsolutions indicate some ongoing showers on Tuesday morning. How \nquickly these showers and residual clouds scour out will determine \nwhether instability is fully realized during the core heating hours. \nAs it stands now, damaging wind gusts and torrential downpours \ncapable of flash flooding are the main threats Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight.\n\nDuring the mid to late week period, the Mid-Atlantic region sits in \na post-frontal environment as the earlier boundary lingers near the \nsoutheastern U.S. Expect reduced shower chances and less humidity as \nthe week progresses. The warmest conditions are likely to be early \nin the week as highs push to near 90 degrees. However, this is \nsimply close to climatology for late July.",
